Found auditing every row shape the lane writes after the World of Tanks
incident. The decide step's reuse-first check asked which of the warm bots'
accounts were still free against tasks in status "active" only. Legacy's
inline reuse asks against "active" OR "planned" (autoFarmer.js, the spokenFor
query beside the reuse record), and the lane's own execute step already
recomputed with both. So for the same inputs a shadow verdict could plan more
reuse accounts than legacy would — a false accounts delta in the comparison —
and in a live cycle a sibling campaign already written as `planned` by
upsertTask was invisible to decide until execute recomputed. Decide and
execute now agree on the number they plan and spend.

One query change. Four tests: a planned sibling's accounts are spoken for; an
active sibling's still are; completed and skipped siblings' are not (legacy
excludes them too); and decide's answer equals what execute would find.

Suite 707/708 vs 703/704; the single failure is unchanged.
